fxsound-linux for Debian and Ubuntu
===================================

Two things about this tree are unusual and both have reasons.


1. debian/ lives under packaging/
---------------------------------

dpkg-buildpackage insists on ./debian at the root of the source tree. This
repository keeps every distribution's packaging under packaging/ — the Arch
PKGBUILD, the two AUR recipes, the desktop entry, the systemd unit — and
putting a bare debian/ at the root next to Cargo.toml would be the one
exception. So: symlink it before building.

    cd /path/to/fxsound-linux
    ln -sfn packaging/debian debian
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

The symlink is in .gitignore's spirit but not in .gitignore; remove it when you
are done, or add it. `dpkg-source` follows the symlink and the resulting source
package has a normal debian/ directory, so nothing downstream sees the
arrangement.


2. The Rust toolchain
---------------------

The long version is at the top of debian/control and it is worth reading before
filing a bug about the build failing. The short version:

  * rust-toolchain.toml pins 1.98.1, and only rustup reads that file.
    debian/rules defaults RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN to stable unless the caller set
    one, so a build host that happens to have rustup installed does not try to
    download a toolchain it cannot reach, while a CI container that installed
    exactly 1.98.1 can still say so.

  * Cargo.toml's `rust-version = "1.98.1"` is enforced by cargo itself against
    any rustc, and nothing in debian/ can get past it.

  * Debian 13 ships rustc 1.85, Ubuntu 24.04 ships 1.75. Neither satisfies the
    floor, so Build-Depends names `rustc (>= 1.98.1~)` and apt will tell you so
    up front rather than letting cargo fail halfway through.

In practice that means building with a toolchain from rustup or from a
container, not from the archive, until a rustc >= 1.98 lands. Do not "fix" it
by lowering rust-version: the floor is there because the code uses edition-2024
and 1.98 features, and lowering it just moves the failure somewhere less
legible.


Building offline
----------------

A builder with no network needs the crates vendored into the source package:

    mkdir -p .cargo
    cargo vendor vendor/ > .cargo/config.toml
    touch vendor/.cargo-checksum-marker

debian/rules adds --offline to the cargo invocation when
vendor/.cargo-checksum-marker exists, so the marker file is the switch. Note
that vendoring is enough for an offline build and is *not* enough for an upload
to the Debian archive, which wants every crate as its own librust-*-dev source
package. That is a different and much larger project.


Building a .deb without dpkg-deb
--------------------------------

packaging/build-deb.sh assembles a working .deb out of an already-built binary
using nothing but ar, tar and gzip. A .deb is an ar archive of three members —
debian-binary, control.tar.gz, data.tar.gz — and that is entirely reproducible
without any part of dpkg being installed. It exists for two situations: a
developer on a non-Debian machine who wants to hand someone a .deb, and a CI
runner where installing debhelper is more trouble than it is worth.

    cargo build --release --bin fxsound
    ./packaging/build-deb.sh

The result lands in dist/. It reads its version from debian/changelog and its
dependencies from debian/control, so the two stay in step, but it is not a
substitute for dpkg-buildpackage: it does no dpkg-shlibdeps, so the library
dependencies it writes have no version bounds, and it builds no source package
and no dbgsym. Run `./packaging/build-deb.sh --help` for the options.


Decisions you might otherwise have to rediscover
------------------------------------------------

* /usr/share/fxsound/presets is not a name this packaging chose. It is
  hardcoded in crates/fxsound-preset/src/store.rs:65 and input.rs:181 as one of
  two search prefixes (the other is /usr/local/share/fxsound). Install the
  presets under a path derived from the package name instead and you get an
  application that starts fine and has no presets.

* The systemd user unit is installed and deliberately not enabled. The desktop
  entry already starts FxSound; an enabled user unit on top of that puts two
  processes in a race for the control socket in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/fxsound. The
  loser exits cleanly, but which one loses depends on the session's startup
  order, which is not a thing to leave to chance. `systemctl --user enable
  --now fxsound` turns it on for anyone who wants it, and debian/*.postrm
  cleans up after them on removal.

* LICENSE is not installed into /usr/share/doc. The Arch package installs it
  because that is Arch's convention; Debian's is debian/copyright, which
  carries the full AGPL-3 text because base-files does not ship AGPL-3 in
  /usr/share/common-licenses (it has GPL-3 and LGPL-3, but not this one).

* There is no postinst. The icon cache and the desktop database are updated by
  dpkg triggers that hicolor-icon-theme and desktop-file-utils declare on
  /usr/share/icons/hicolor and /usr/share/applications; shipping files there is
  all that is needed to activate them.

* Almost the whole graphics stack is dlopen()ed rather than linked.
  `objdump -p usr/bin/fxsound` lists five NEEDED entries; libEGL, libGL, the
  Wayland client libraries, libxkbcommon and the X11 libraries are strings
  inside the binary that libloading resolves at startup. dpkg-shlibdeps cannot
  see any of them, which is why debian/control names them by hand. If the
  package ever installs cleanly and then fails to open a window, that list is
  the first place to look.

